Pure virtual functions in C++ are a type of function declared in a base class that makes it abstract, forcing derived classes to provide an implementation for this function. They provide a framework for subclasses to fill in, as they must be overridden in any non-abstract class that directly or indirectly inherits from the class where they are declared.

Polymorphism is a feature of OOP (Object Oriented Programming) that helps in performing a single action in different ways. In C++, we can achieve polymorphism via function overloading, operator overloading, and virtual functions.

Here is an example:

```C++
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

// Abstract Class
class Shape {
public:
    // Pure Virtual Function
    virtual float getArea() = 0; // Must be overridden in any non-abstract class
};

// Derived Class
class Circle: public Shape {
    float radius;
public:
    Circle(float r) : radius(r) {}
    // Implementation of pure virtual function in base class
    float getArea() {
        return 3.14159 * radius * radius;
    }
};

// Derived Class
class Rectangle: public Shape {
    float width, height;
public:
    Rectangle(float w, float h) : width(w), height(h) {}
    // Implementation of pure virtual function in base class
    float getArea() {
        return width * height;
    }
};

int main() {
    Circle circle(5);
    Rectangle rectangle(3,4);

    //Pointer of Shape type
    Shape* shape;
    shape = &circle;
    cout << "Area of Circle: " << shape->getArea() << endl; // Using Polymorphism
    shape = &rectangle;
    cout << "Area of Rectangle: " << shape->getArea() << endl; // Using Polymorphism

    return 0;
}
```

The main benefit of pure virtual functions and polymorphism is that they allow us to work at a higher level of abstraction. 

A high level of abstraction allows for more complex higher-level operations and complex communication with other entities, such as functions and data. An additional benefit is that objects of different but related types can be treated as objects of a basic type in their inheritance hierarchy, leading to code that is simpler and easier to understand and maintain.

